 \begin{abstract}
-This paper presents the Object-Driven Cellular Automata (ODCA) framework, a traffic microsimulation paradigm that combines the spatial simplicity of cellular automata with discrete event simulation. Each vehicle is an asynchronous process that acquires cells through a request--wait--seize--delay--release protocol, so congestion emerges from resource contention rather than from explicit gap rules, and the delayed release produces headways consistent with Newell's car-following model, yielding a triangular fundamental diagram without calibration. Drivers re-evaluate speed and direction when neighbours move rather than at fixed intervals, so computational effort concentrates where interactions occur. We derive closed-form expressions linking the protocol parameters to capacity, critical density and backward wave speed, and extend them to mixed autonomous and human-driven traffic. In 20 replications on a four-lane freeway offered 7{,}000\,veh/h, raising the autonomous share from 0\% to 70\% increases served throughput by 18.9\% (from $5{,}874 \pm 19$ to $6{,}983 \pm 34$\,veh/h, 95\% CI) and cuts average delay by 95\% (from $217 \pm 6$ to $10.2 \pm 0.3$\,s). Throughput understates the capacity effect once the network serves 99.8\% of the demand offered, after which the benefit appears as delay reduction, at a lane-drop bottleneck as well.
+Cellular automata have been simulated on discrete-event engines since Cell-DEVS, but in that lineage the cell is the active model and the moving object is a value passed between cell states. This paper inverts that assignment for traffic microsimulation. In the Object-Driven Cellular Automata (ODCA) framework each vehicle is an asynchronous process and each cell is a passive resource of capacity one, so behaviour attaches to the object that has it and the lattice carries none. A vehicle acquires cells through a request--wait--seize--delay--release protocol, congestion emerges from resource contention rather than from cell rules or explicit gap checks, and speed is a real number rather than a discrete cell state, which removes the integer-speed artifact that survives the move to discrete events. The delayed release produces headways consistent with Newell's car-following model, yielding a triangular fundamental diagram without calibration. Drivers re-evaluate speed and direction when neighbours move rather than at fixed intervals, so computational effort concentrates where interactions occur. We derive closed-form expressions linking the protocol parameters to capacity, critical density and backward wave speed, and extend them to mixed autonomous and human-driven traffic. In 20 replications on a four-lane freeway offered 7{,}000\,veh/h, raising the autonomous share from 0\% to 70\% increases served throughput by 18.9\% (from $5{,}874 \pm 19$ to $6{,}983 \pm 34$\,veh/h, 95\% CI) and cuts average delay by 95\% (from $217 \pm 6$ to $10.2 \pm 0.3$\,s). Throughput understates the capacity effect once the network serves 99.8\% of the demand offered, after which the benefit appears as delay reduction, at a lane-drop bottleneck as well.
 \end{abstract}
 
